WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.203
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.203 --> 00:00:03.870
TINGTING ZHOU: My
name is Tingting Zhou,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.620
and I'm a neuroscientist at
the McGovern Institute at MIT.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.080
Imagine for a second
that you are a mouse,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.330
and you'd really like to
eat a piece of cheese.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.400
But in order to
get the cheese, you

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.530
must remember the
color of this light.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:20.010
You can't forget it, even
if the light turns off.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.320
Right now, your mouse
brain is holding

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.420
this important
information in an area

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.700
called the prefrontal cortex.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.140
And then when you
have to remember

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.110
which light you saw earlier,
you probably guessed correctly.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.150
This is called working memory.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:36.570
The mice in my lab have
difficulty with working memory.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.730
They have schizophrenia,
a serious brain

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.790
disorder that affects the
ability to think clearly.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:45.180
They cannot remember information
for more than a few seconds

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.450
because they have mistakes in
their DNA and their prefrontal

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:52.080
cortex doesn't connect properly
to other parts of the brain.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.320
I'm studying how we can fix the
genes and the abnormal brain

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.340
connections associated with
schizophrenia so that all mice

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.950
and people can live
a rewarding life.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

